htget for the Microsoft TCP/IP sockets interface
================================================

htget.com fetches one URL over the MS TCP/IP stack of MS Network
Client 3.0 or LAN Manager, which is an NDIS protocol sitting on top of
an NDIS MAC driver - dosemu's built-in one, for instance.

The point of it is testing: the tests in test/ndis drive the MAC driver
from a stand-in protocol written against it, so they prove nothing
about how it behaves under the real Microsoft protocols. htget takes
the other route - real protman.dos, real tcpdrv.dos, real tcptsr - and
the only unusual thing in the whole stack is the MAC driver underneath.

    usage: htget <dotted-ip> [<port>] [<path>]

The response, headers and all, goes to stdout, so it can be redirected
to a file; progress and errors go to stderr. There is no name
resolution: give it an address. For example, with a web server on the
slirp gateway:

    htget 10.0.2.2 80 /index.html

Loading the stack
-----------------

In the boot drive's userhook.sys, in this order:

    DEVICE=C:\NET\PROTMAN.DOS /I:C:\NET
    DEVICE=D:\DOSEMU\NDIS.SYS
    DEVICE=C:\NET\TCPDRV.DOS /I:C:\NET

and then, from autoexec.bat or by hand:

    C:\NET\NETBIND.COM
    C:\NET\TCPTSR.EXE
    C:\NET\SOCKETS.EXE

PROTOCOL.INI needs a section for the MAC driver and TCPIP bound to it,
see src/doc/README/ndis. sockets.exe is the piece that provides the
interface below; socktsr.exe from older LAN Manager releases does too.

The interface
-------------

The sockets TSR is reached through the TCPDRV$ character device and a
far entry point that it hands out. No specification of any of this
seems to have been published, so it was recovered from DOS_SOCK.LIB of
the Microsoft TCP/IP Sockets Development Kit 1.0 - the library that MS
C programs linked against. The numbers are in mstcp.h; below is where
each one comes from, so that the reading can be checked rather than
taken on trust. Nothing is copied from the SDK: this is a description
of an interface, and the code here was written from it.

The library module solocate.asm holds the whole locator:

  _locater_open    opens "TCPDRV$" with INT 21h AX=3D00h.

  _locater_bind    puts 2 in the first byte of a 25-byte block, the
                   caller's word in the third, and passes it to the
                   device with INT 21h AX=4402h, CX=25. It then takes
                   the status from the second byte and a far pointer
                   from bytes 4..7, which is the entry point.

  _locater_unbind  the same with 3 in the first byte.

  _locater_send    loads ES:BX with the caller's request block and
                   does a far call through the pointer that
                   _locater_bind stored. So the entry point takes its
                   argument in ES:BX, not on the stack - sockutil.c
                   installs _locater_send itself as the "driver entry"
                   that the socket calls go through.

The word in the block is the process id: sockutil.c fills it from
_so_get_DOS_pid, which is the PSP of the process, and the TSR keeps
its state per process. A process that binds should unbind again.

Every socket call is one request block, passed to the entry point:

    offset 0   byte   opcode
    offset 1   dword  far pointer to the caller's errno word
    offset 5   dword  far pointer to the caller's result word
    offset 9   word   the process id, again
    offset 11         the arguments of this particular call

which is what each of socket.c, connect.c, send.c, recv.c and close.c
in the library builds on its stack before calling. The opcodes and
arguments are taken from the same modules:

    socket.c    0x10   word domain, word type, word protocol
    close.c     0x02   word s
    connect.c   0x04   word s, far name, word namelen
    send.c      0x0d   word s, far buf, word len, word flags,
                       far to, word tolen, byte 0
    recv.c      0x0b   word s, far buf, word len, word flags,
                       far from, far fromlen, byte 2

send and recv are sendto and recvfrom underneath; the trailing byte
picks the flavour, and send.c passes a null destination with a length
of 16 while recv.c passes null pointers.

The result is not returned in a register: it is written through the
pointer at offset 5, negative on failure, with the error at offset 1.
The error numbers are in sock_err.h of the SDK.

What is not replicated here is the rest of what the library's
so_drvropen does after binding - it installs INT 24h and 2Fh handlers
so that an abnormally terminating program still unbinds, and it makes
one "get status" call whose result it only uses to answer its own
"is this a compatible version" question. htget does neither, and
unbinds on every path out instead.

Testing
-------

tsockets.sys is a TCPDRV$ device that speaks the interface above and
answers from canned data. It is what the test_mstcp_htget cases of the
test suite run htget against, and all it can show is that htget uses
the interface the way this file describes it. Whether that description
matches Microsoft's TSR is not something any test here can answer -
that takes a machine with MS Network Client on it.
